# meta::genre = bio
# meta::salientEntities = 3 (5*), 24 (5*), 86 (5*), 103 (3), 1 (2*), 61 (2*), 87 (2), 90 (2*), 2 (1), 4 (1*), 5 (1*), 8 (1), 10 (1), 23 (1), 26 (1), 37 (1*), 89 (1*), 97 (1), 105 (1), 118 (1), 120 (1)
# meta::sourceURL = https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lord_Byron
# meta::speakerCount = 0
# meta::summary1 = (human1) This section gives some details of Lord Byron's early life including his education at Aberdeen Grammar School, Harrow and Trinity College, as well as his romantic involvements and friendships with Mary Chaworth, John FitzGibbon and John Edleston.
# meta::summary2 = (human2) Byron was an English poet in the early 1800s who had relationships with younger boys during his time at the Harrow school and Trinity College in Cambridge during a time in England where being convicted of suspected of homosexuality could have resulted in consequences as serious as public hanging, which are chronicled in his poems such as Childish Recollections and Thyrza.
# meta::summary3 = (human3) Byron started school in Dulwich but was sent to Harrow in 1801, where he met and fell in love with Mary Chaworth and settled into friendships and romantic relationships with John FitzGibbon and John Thomas Claridge, before attending Trinity College in 1805 where he formed a relationship with John Edleston and lifelong friendships with John Cam Hobhouse and Francis Hodgson.
# meta::summary4 = (human4) Byron attended Harrow school and Trinity College during the nineteenth century, where he formed friendships despite his violence, as well as romantic and possibly sexual relationships with other boys, which continued into adulthood during a time in England which severely sanctioned homosexuality.
# meta::summary5 = (human5) Byron, throughout his education at Harrow, Trinity College, and Cambridge, experienced and wrote about his diverse early loves with both men and women during a time in which homosexuality was a hangable offense, reflecting his intense emotionality and the complex sociopolitical landscape of his youth.
# meta::summaryClosed1 = (humanC1) Byron's formal education was marked by tumultuous romantic relationships, mostly with fellow male schoolmates, some of which he described in his memoirs and poetry.
# meta::summaryClosed2 = (humanC2) This excerpt from Lord Byron's biography describes his school years in the 1800s at Aberdeen Grammar School and Harrow, where his violent romantic affairs started when he fell in love with Mary Chaworth, followed by a number of presumed homosexual relationships with John FitzGibbon and other boys at Harrow and during his time at Trinity College, Cambridge.
# meta::summaryClosed3 = (humanC3) Byron's early life included grammar school (1799) and Harrow (1801-5), an absence in 1803-4 due to his love for Mary Charowoth, but after returning he made friends and a likely romantic relationship with a schoolmate, while at Trinity college (1805) he finds an associate "protege", in a period influenced by lack of moderation and discipline in schooling and relationships.
