# meta::sourceURL = https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emperor_Norton
# meta::speakerCount = 0
# meta::summary1 = (human1) Joshua Norton was an eccentric resident of San Francisco who proclaimed himself Emperor of the United States in the second half of the 19th Century, and came to be known as Emperor Norton.
# meta::summary2 = (human2) Joshua Abraham Norton (c. 1818-January 8, 1880), also known as Emperor Norton, was a resident of San Francisco who lost his fortune in Peruvian rice but was still celebrated by other citizens of San Francisco for his regal presence and his numerous decrees calling for a bridge and tunnel connecting San Francisco to Oakland.
# meta::summary3 = (human3) Joshua Abraham Norton was a citizen of San Francisco who proclaimed himself Emperor of the United States and Protector of Mexico and issued numerous decrees such as his order to dissolve the US Congress even though he didn't have any real authority, and despite his eccentricism, citizens of San Francisco still celebrated his regal presence.
# meta::summary4 = (human4) Joshua Abraham Norton, otherwise known as Emperor Norton, was a 19th century San Francisco, California resident best known for proclaiming himself “Emperor of the United States” and “Protector of Mexico” who was celebrated by San Francisco citizens, and who wrote a number of decrees including one to abolish the United States Congress.
# meta::summary5 = (human5) Joshua Norton, who lived in the 1800s, was from England and South Africa but then moved to San Francisco, where he proclaimed in several newspapers that he was now emperor of the United States and that Congress would be dissolved; his reign was mostly unrecognized but he is still remembered in literature.
# meta::summaryClosed1 = (humanC1) Joshua Edward Norton was an English-born citizen of San Francisco who in 1859, after losing his fortune in rice investment, declared himself Emperor of the United States as a means to improve on what he saw as a corrupt government, and despite having no actual authority beyond a light-hearted deference shown to him by San Franciscans, issued decrees such as abolishing Congress.
