# newdoc id = GUM_speech_impeachment
# global.Entity = GRP-etype-infstat-salience-centering-minspan-link-identity
# meta::author = Steny Hoyer
# meta::dateCollected = 2020-09-04
# meta::dateCreated = 2020-02-05
# meta::dateModified = 2020-02-05
# meta::genre = speech
# meta::salientEntities = 3 (5*), 6 (5*), 7 (5*), 53 (3*), 1 (2), 2 (2*), 5 (2*), 11 (2), 45 (2), 46 (2*), 49 (2*), 75 (2), 8 (1), 10 (1), 12 (1), 17 (1), 18 (1), 19 (1), 47 (1*), 50 (1), 51 (1), 54 (1*), 58 (1*), 60 (1*), 61 (1*), 63 (1), 72 (1*), 87 (1), 88 (1), 91 (1), 92 (1), 103 (1)
# meta::sourceURL = https://www.congress.gov/congressional-record/2020/02/05/house-section/article/H776-2
# meta::speakerCount = 1
# meta::summary1 = (human1) In a speech in the US Congress, a Democratic member of Congress accuses Republican Senators of failing to fulfill their oath to conduct an impartial trial in the impeachment of President Donald Trump for abuse of power and attempts to solicit foreign interference in the 2020 elections.
# meta::summary2 = (human2) The politician making this speech established the precedent of setting aside partisanship to chastise Republican senators who failed to vote against the impeachment of Donald Trump, stating that history would not look back fondly upon those who lacked the courage to do what is right.
# meta::summary3 = (human3) In a speech to the Speaker of the House, a Representative discusses an upcoming vote in the US Senate on two Articles of Impeachment (abuse of power and obstruction of Congress) for US President Donald John Trump, expressing his concern that Republican Senators will not vote impartially because they voted to block witnesses and evidence.
# meta::summary4 = (human4) In a speech addressed to the Speaker of the House, with the Senate’s vote on the two Articles of Impeachment the House presented against then President Donald Trump approaching, a U.S. Representative argues the Senate will fail their jobs and the Constitution considering their inability to remain impartial in the impeachment process.
# meta::summary5 = (human5) In a speech on the trial of the impeachment of Donald Trump, President of the United States, one person recalls the oath taken by senators at the first 1868 impeachment of a president to do impartial justice according to the Constitution and laws, and states that the House did its job during impeachment, but an unfair trial resulted from senators' choosing party over country.
# meta::summaryClosed1 = (humanC1) A member of the United States House of Representatives describes why the House voted to impeach President Donald Trump for election interference and withholding military aid to Ukraine, praising the careful and fact-driven manner with which the articles were brought and argued within the House, and admonishing Republican senators for refusing to vote in favor of impeachment.
# meta::title = AMERICANS WILL JUDGE
