# newdoc id = GUM_textbook_governments
# global.Entity = GRP-etype-infstat-salience-centering-minspan-link-identity
# meta::author = Glen Krutz and Sylvie Waskiewicz
# meta::dateCollected = 2021-09-08
# meta::dateCreated = 2019-02-21
# meta::dateModified = 2020-12-01
# meta::genre = textbook
# meta::salientEntities = 1 (5*), 2 (5*), 115 (5*), 132 (5*), 39 (4), 64 (4*), 145 (4), 4 (3*), 6 (3*), 67 (3*), 147 (3*), 5 (2), 15 (2), 36 (2), 40 (2), 45 (2), 138 (2*), 155 (2*), 156 (2), 7 (1), 14 (1), 35 (1), 46 (1), 63 (1), 114 (1), 126 (1), 127 (1*), 139 (1), 141 (1*)
# meta::sourceURL = https://openstax.org/books/american-government-2e/pages/1-1-what-is-government
# meta::speakerCount = 0
# meta::summary1 = (human1) This section of a textbook explains and exemplifies different types of government, including representative democracy as in the United States, direct democracy as in ancient Athens, monarchy as in Saudi Arabia, oligarchy as in Cuba's reigning Communist Party, and totalitarianism as in North Korea.
# meta::summary2 = (human2) This section of the textbook explains the different types of government, first describing representative democracy and how it balances majority rule and minority protection, using the US as an example, before describing direct democracy, using ancient Athens as an example, monarchy, and oligarchy, and totalitarianism.
# meta::summary3 = (human3) This textbook section describes different types of governments, comparing a republic, in which the people elect representatives to reflect the opinions of the majority but has safeguards in place to protect the rights of the minority, to a democracy as well as contrasting these with a monarchy, oligarchy, and totalitarianism.
# meta::summary4 = (human4) This section of a textbook on different types of government provides explanations and examples of representative democracies like the United States, direct democracies like ancient Athens, monarchies like many southwest Asian kingdoms, oligarchies like Cuba and China, and totalitarian governments like North Korea.
# meta::summary5 = (human5) This textbook chapter explains different types of governments, including republics, where citizens elect representatives to govern on their behalf while protecting minority rights, direct democracy, where citizens could directly participate in countrywide decision-making, and other elite-driven forms of government such as monarchies, oligarchies, and totalitarian governments.
# meta::summaryClosed1 = (humanC1) The chapter outlines different types of government, such as representative and direct democracies, monarchies, oligarchies and totalitarian regimes, while also providing examples of such governments across the world and throughout history.
# meta::summaryClosed2 = (humanC2) The text explains different types of governments based on power structure, such as democracy in the US, a representative democracy with elements of direct democracy, contrasted with oligarchy where an elite majority has power (Political Party, Royal Family), and rarer totalitarianism where government is more important than the people and limits their rights (e.g North Korea).
# meta::summaryClosed3 = (humanC3) This website shows four different types of government: representative democracy, as in the US, where representative governments support majority rule but protect minority rule; direct democracy, where people participate directly, like in ancient Athens; monarchy, there is only one ruler, like in Arabia; and totalitarianism, where the government controls all, as in North Korea.
