# meta::sourceURL = https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Athens
# meta::speakerCount = 0
# meta::summary1 = (human1) This article presents Athens, an ancient city and capital of modern Greece with a metropolitan population of 3.7 million, which hosted the 2004 Olympic games and features archeological sites and restored neoclassical as well as post-modern buildings, and is best visited in spring and late autumn.
# meta::summary2 = (human2) Athens (population 3.7 million), the capital of Greece and the "birthplace of...Western Civilization," has a rich political and social history, numerous architectural landmarks in the historical center, and a vehicle-induced pollution problem exacerbated by geography.
# meta::summary3 = (human3) This article describes Athens, the capital city of Greece, its history spanning from its settlement in 3000 BC through the modern Greek state, its blend of classical, neoclassical, and postmodernist architecture, and its renovations and infrastructure improvements related to the 2004 Summer Olympic Games.
# meta::summary4 = (human4) Athens, the capital city of Greece, has a long and still developing history, stretching from the first pre-historic settlements of Old Athens, to the hosting of the modern Summer Olympic games of 2004, to the present day city, which has continued to expand and now boasts both historic neoclassical buildings and post-modern buildings.
# meta::summary5 = (human5) This guide to Athens traces its evolution from a prehistoric settlement to its modern identity in the 21st century, highlighting the city's architectural transformation, including major infrastructural improvements and renovations that enhanced quality of life, and detailing relevant climate information to best inform travel decisions.
# meta::summaryClosed1 = (humanC1) This travel guide for Athens, Greece details its history as the birthplace of Western civilization and democracy as well as its decline under the Ottoman Empire and subsequent redevelopment in the late 20th century, culminating in its hosting the 2004 Olympic Games and becoming a tourist destination best enjoyed around early autumn.
